\begin_layout Title
Brownian motion and stochastic integrals,
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

viewed from
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Ito's isometry and Girsanov's theorem
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

[DRAFT.
 Thoughts only]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two dual approaches to this that I've observed.
 One concerns Stochastic processes,
 the other —
 distributions.
 In this article I try to carry out a pedagogical presentation of material studied at uni,
 i.e.
 in a way that feels as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

natural
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as possible rather than forced.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Ultra Basics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Brownian motion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Brownian motion is defined either
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
as a stochastic process 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(W_{t}\right)_{t\ge0}$
\end_inset

 satisfying three properties:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $W_{0}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 is normally distributed with variance 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

;
 and all increments (of the same length) 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{s,t}$
\end_inset

 share that same distribution;
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Later I will write 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}$
\end_inset

 to mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{0,t}$
\end_inset

,
 so just 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

,
 but written in the increment style to stress that we are interested in increments of a particular length.
 By stationarity,
 expected values then are the same for increments not started at zero,
 too.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For 
\begin_inset Formula $s<t$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{s}\perp\Delta W_{s,t}$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 independent increments
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
as a limiting distribution on the space of continuous functions 
\begin_inset Formula $C(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^{n})$
\end_inset

 of the distributions of random walks on uniform grid 
\begin_inset Formula $\{t_{i}\coloneqq i/n\}_{i=0}^{n}$
\end_inset

 with jump 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\sqrt{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first view explains how 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 is moving in space,
 the second —
 why the variance is 
\begin_inset Formula $1/t$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 why deviation is 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\sqrt{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Stochastic integral for simple functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\dP}{\text{d}\mathbb{P}}
{\text{d}\mathbb{P}}
\end_inset


\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\dW}{\text{d}W}
\end_inset


\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\d}{\mathrm{d}}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a Lebesgue-Stieltjes integral style we can integrate simple predictable functions against the brownian motion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Remember that the Lebesgue-Stieltjes integral against a function (of bounded variation) 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int_{X}f\d g
\]

\end_inset

works similarly to the standard Lebesgue integral but instead of using a 
\emph on
measure 
\emph default
for the floor increments (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(x-y)$
\end_inset

) when infinitesimally multiplying the integrated function,
 it uses the provided function 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

's increment,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta g=g(x)-g(y)$
\end_inset

,
 to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

measure
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A simple process is a process taking finitely many values that we denote 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1},\ldots,f_{k}$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 it is expressible as a finite sum
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f^{\omega}(t)=\sum_{n}f_{n}^{\omega}\textbf{1}_{\left[t_{n},t_{n+1}\right]}(t)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So we define the stochastic integral against the brownian motion 
\begin_inset Formula $\dW_{s}$
\end_inset

 of simple predictable functions as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int_{0}^{t}f(s)\dW_{s}\coloneqq\sum_{n}f_{n}\Delta W_{n}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Square of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

output is expected to be the 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

input
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are a range of facts that I summarize under this section's title.
 The chaos of the material is vastly reduced by viewing many results as an instance of this pattern.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Of 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 itself
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Discretely
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second view of Brownian motion above also tells us that considering one jump of the random walk,
 say 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\text{time: } & t_{i}\to t_{i+1}\\
\text{space: } & \xi_{i}\to\xi_{i+1}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

With the choice 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t_{i}=1/n$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\xi_{i}$
\end_inset

 is either 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus the square 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\xi_{i}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is most definitely 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{n}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta\xi_{i}^{2}=\Delta t_{t}
\]

\end_inset

 —
 the square of the output's increment equals the input increment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Continuously
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the limiting distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{s,t}$
\end_inset

 is definitely does not square to just 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(t-s\right)$
\end_inset

,
 but it is interesting that its mean 
\emph on
does
\emph default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{E}\left(\Delta W_{t}^{2}\right)=t.
\]

\end_inset

 We see this from the property charaterising the distribution of the (non-squared) increment 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left(\Delta W_{t}^{2}\right) & \equiv\int_{\Omega}\Delta W_{t}^{2}\dP=\int_{\mathbb{R}}y^{2}\dP_{Z}\\
 & =\int_{\mathbb{R}}y^{2}\Phi(y)\d y\overset{!}{=}\sigma^{2}\int_{\mathbb{R}}\Phi(y)\d y=t
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where in 
\begin_inset Formula $\overset{!}{=}$
\end_inset

 we used (
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 denoting the variance of the normal variable,
 i.e.
 here just 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

;
 also omitting integral signs)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
y^{2}e^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}\d y & ={\color{blue}-2\sigma^{2}y}e^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}\d y^{{\color{blue}2}}/{\color{blue}-2\sigma^{2}}\\
 & =-y\d e^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}\\
 & =-ye^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}+e^{-y^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}\d y.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Thoughts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note:::::
 I can't quite explain yet why in the discrete case randomness goes away (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\xi_{i}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is not random),
 while in the continuous – not (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is still random).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Of the Ito integral for simple functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Above we computed the expected square of the raw increments 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta W_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 In a martingale-transformation fashion we can consider the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

transformed
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 increments 
\begin_inset Formula $f\Delta W$
\end_inset

 and their integral's expected square:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\int_{0}^{t}f(s)\dW_{s}\right)^{2}\right] & \equiv\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\sum\nolimits_{n}f_{n}\Delta W_{n}\right)^{2}\right]\\
 & \overset{\text{lin}}{=}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n}f_{n}^{2}\Delta W_{n}^{2}\right]+\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n\neq m}f_{n}f_{m}\Delta W_{n}\Delta W_{m}\right]\\
 & \overset{\perp}{=}\sum_{n}\mathbb{E}\left[f_{n}^{2}\right]\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta W_{n}^{2}\right]}_{\Delta t}+\cancel{2\sum_{n<m}\mathbb{E}\left[f_{n}f_{m}\Delta W_{n}\right]\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta W_{m}\right]}_{0}}\\
 & =\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n}f_{n}^{2}\Delta t\right]\\
 & \equiv\mathop{\mathbb{E}}_{\omega}\left[\int_{0}^{t}f_{\omega}^{2}(s)\d s\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset

which I roughly interpret as
\end_layout

